# Fallback log

This is the project's canonical debug record for deliberate fallback and degraded
behavior. It is deliberately empty at scaffold time. No entry means no fallback is
approved.

Read owner rule G2.8 before adding an entry or troubleshooting a possible fallback.
A fallback is permitted only when it remains distinct from success, visible when
appropriate, testable, approved, and linked to a discharge gate. Add its entry before
adding the fallback code.

For each fallback, create a heading using its stable ID and record all of these fields:

1. Status: proposed, active, deferred, or discharged.
2. Exact trigger.
3. Affected operation and safe resource.
4. Why the operation needs that resource.
5. Unavailable data or capability.
6. Visible behavior.
7. Runtime diagnostic signature, including the same stable fallback ID.
8. Test and exact evidence location.
9. Owner or scoped owner ruling.
10. Removal condition and discharge gate.
11. Activation evidence or the declared diagnostic sink that records activations.

Do not record an ordinary exception as a fallback. Do not use an entry to justify an
empty catch, false success, invented value, or silent continuation. During
troubleshooting, compare the observed trigger and diagnostic ID with this file before
classifying the symptom. If no registered fallback matches, investigate it as a
genuine error.
